\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces WP-REPAIR-2 with the unit definition enforced by design: $\eta {=}0$ is emitted once (the target is direction-free) with $24$ optimiser seeds, $\eta {>}0$ has $8$ directions $\times $ $24$ seeds $=192$ units. Holm is applied within the $\eta {=}0$ and $\eta {=}1$ families. Reading: the repaired arm does beat the \texttt  {original} and the random-direction control at $\eta {=}0$, but the \emph  {gate-count-matched duplicate} control closes most of that gap, so the symmetry-specific part ($-0.0006$) is not established. At $\eta {>}0$ the cost of enforced invariance is robust and grows with the optimiser budget.}}{10}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:rep2}{{4}{10}{WP-REPAIR-2 with the unit definition enforced by design: $\eta {=}0$ is emitted once (the target is direction-free) with $24$ optimiser seeds, $\eta {>}0$ has $8$ directions $\times $ $24$ seeds $=192$ units. Holm is applied within the $\eta {=}0$ and $\eta {=}1$ families. Reading: the repaired arm does beat the \texttt {original} and the random-direction control at $\eta {=}0$, but the \emph {gate-count-matched duplicate} control closes most of that gap, so the symmetry-specific part ($-0.0006$) is not established. At $\eta {>}0$ the cost of enforced invariance is robust and grows with the optimiser budget}{table.4}{}}
